WEBVTT

00:00:00.551 --> 00:00:07.607
All about India, by Philippa-Tracy, chapter 1, ancient and medieval history.

00:00:07.666 --> 00:00:09.508
Greetings!

00:00:09.568 --> 00:00:12.050
I am the spirit of Ashukha the Great.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:21.466
In the 3rd century BC, I ruled for almost four decades over the Morian Empire, the largest empire in India.

00:00:21.526 --> 00:00:29.001
My emblem, the Ashukka chakra wheel, is in the center of the Indian flag and it symbolizes movement.

00:00:29.061 --> 00:00:35.534
Like a wheel, India is always in motion, constantly changing and making progress.

00:00:35.594 --> 00:00:49.340
The Indian subcontinent got its name from the word Indus, because its first civilization, the Indus Valley civilization, lived on the Indus River, in today's Pakistan and northwest India.

00:00:49.399 --> 00:00:58.110
Being Emperor of India was not an easy job, as the country was so enormous, about 13 times the size of the UK.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:04.993
For centuries, India was divided into lots of small kingdoms, each governed by its own ruler.

00:01:05.053 --> 00:01:10.716
This led to political problems, as the rulers didn't always see things the same way.

00:01:10.776 --> 00:01:18.818
With so many rulers governing at the same time, it was very difficult for the Emperor to keep universal power over his empire.

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:28.974
empires rose and empires fell, some lasted decades or centuries, while others quickly disintegrated because they had weak rulers.

00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:38.988
All these rulers changed the face of India in some way, but only one of them succeeded in uniting India's kingdoms into one state.

00:01:39.048 --> 00:01:47.420
His name was Chandragupta Moria, and he was the powerful and strong warrior who founded the Morian dynasty.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:51.800
He ruled as the first Mauryan emperor of India from 321 to 297 BC.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:56.631
By the way, he was also my grandfather.

00:01:56.691 --> 00:02:06.487
When Chandra Gupta died, my father, Bin Dusara, followed him and ruled from 297 to 272 or 3 BC.

00:02:06.507 --> 00:02:15.763
He had 16 wives and many children, which meant there was a lot of competition for the throne.

00:02:15.822 --> 00:02:26.563
After his death, the fighting for the throne continued for four long years until I finally got the most support.

00:02:26.623 --> 00:02:38.527
I took my father's place as Emperor in 268 BC and ruled until 232 BC, expanding India's territories even more.

00:02:38.587 --> 00:02:44.735
I believed in Buddhism and dedicated my life to spreading this religion in my country.

00:02:44.795 --> 00:02:56.840
I was considered one of the most powerful emperors of the Indian subcontinent in ancient times, and mine was one of the most glorious and prosperous periods in Indian history.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.900
Maybe that's why my emblem appears on the modern Indian flag.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:08.281
When I died in 232 BC, my people were inconsolable.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:14.734
According to legend, at my cremation, my body burnt for seven days and seven nights.

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:18.296
People saw this as a sign of my greatness.

00:03:18.356 --> 00:03:28.872
Unfortunately, all good things come to an end, and the Morian Empire fell when the last of the Morian rulers was killed in 185 BC.

00:03:28.932 --> 00:03:44.418
However, the end of one era marks the beginning of another, and there were many other emperors, not as great as my grandfather and I if I may say so, but who were also admirable and good for the country.

00:03:44.478 --> 00:03:56.201
Between the fourth and sixth centuries AD, India experienced its golden age, with powerful rulers like Chandragupta I and Chandragupta II.

00:03:56.261 --> 00:04:04.484
This was a time of many achievements in the fields of engineering, arts, science, technology and religion.

00:04:04.544 --> 00:04:11.200
During this period, chess was invented and a monetary system was used for the first time.

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:14.412
Later, impressive temples were built.

00:04:14.472 --> 00:04:23.315
Already in the 11th century AD, builders managed to move an 82,000 kg stone to the top of a temple.

00:04:23.375 --> 00:04:25.963
How did they do that amazing thing?

00:04:26.022 --> 00:04:28.093
We still don't know.

00:04:28.153 --> 00:04:36.515
Archaeological evidence shows that water conservation for irrigation was another important achievement of ancient India.

00:04:36.575 --> 00:04:41.106
Can you believe that people still use this ancient system today?

00:04:41.166 --> 00:04:45.047
Now let's move on to another important empire.

00:04:45.107 --> 00:04:49.864
Have you ever wondered why so many Indians are also Muslims?

00:04:49.924 --> 00:04:56.832
The first Muslims arrived in India in the 8th century and slowly began to conquer territory.

00:04:56.892 --> 00:05:01.983
Muslim invaders founded the Mughal Empire in 1526.

00:05:02.043 --> 00:05:12.710
They were a strong military power and expanded their empire to three times its size by the 1560s, during the reign of Akbar the Great.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:22.689
The Mughals also built impressive monuments like the Taj Mahal and the Red Fort and introduced an effective tax collection system.

00:05:22.748 --> 00:05:29.060
Although they were Muslims, they were influenced by the Hindus and respected their culture.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:33.792
Unfortunately, not all the Mughal emperors were like Akbar.

00:05:33.852 --> 00:05:39.303
Many of them were useless rulers who killed their own families to win the throne.

00:05:39.363 --> 00:05:45.436
Orangzeb, the sixth emperor of the Mughal Empire, was particularly cruel.

00:05:45.496 --> 00:05:53.689
He imprisoned his father and ordered his servants to cut off his elder brother's head, which he sent to his father in a basket.

00:05:53.749 --> 00:05:59.772
despite his cruelty, Aurangzeb was the last of the great emperors of the Mughal dynasty.

00:05:59.832 --> 00:06:11.231
By the time he died, after ruling for almost 49 years, 1658-1707, the Mughal Empire was already disintegrating.

00:06:11.291 --> 00:06:17.693
His death marked the end of an era and the beginning of the modern or colonial period.

00:06:17.753 --> 00:06:28.828
There were other emperors after him, but the wars of succession and bad administration led to the collapse of the Mughal Empire in the mid-18th century.

00:06:28.888 --> 00:06:39.474
I would love to share more of India's fascinating ancient and medieval history with you, but I must continue in my search for self-knowledge, so I'm leaving now.

00:06:39.534 --> 00:06:46.586
It's time for you to learn about India's modern history, Mahatma Gandhi and India's fight for independence.

00:06:46.646 --> 00:06:57.704
CHAPTER II THE ROAD TO INDEPENDENCE colonial rule In the 1600s in Europe, tea and spices were in high demand.

00:06:57.764 --> 00:07:05.501
India produced these, so Queen Elizabeth I of England sent merchants to start a trading company in India.

00:07:05.561 --> 00:07:09.422
The British East India Company, EIC.

00:07:09.482 --> 00:07:13.304
It controlled all trade between England and Asia.

00:07:13.364 --> 00:07:24.811
For more than 100 years, the company traded mostly with India buying cheap cotton cloth, silk, sugar and spices, which they sold for a lot of money.

00:07:24.871 --> 00:07:38.942
The Mughal emperors permitted the British to build factories in Bombay, Madras and Calcutta, and as more and more British traders went to live near the factories, these areas developed into colonies.

00:07:39.002 --> 00:07:43.158
The Marathas and growing British influence.

00:07:43.218 --> 00:07:47.994
The Maratas were Hindu warriors who were at war with the Mughals.

00:07:48.054 --> 00:08:01.508
They had existed since the 14th century, but as the Mughal Empire continued to decline, they started to become more powerful and they established their own Maratha Empire in 1674.

00:08:01.568 --> 00:08:12.492
After the last Mughal emperor with any real power died in 1748, the Maratas easily took control of most of northern India.

00:08:12.552 --> 00:08:15.324
However, they didn't have an emperor.

00:08:15.383 --> 00:08:20.936
They had several Peshwas, prime ministers governing different areas.

00:08:20.996 --> 00:08:28.684
This weakened the power of the Maratha empire, making it easier for the growing British population to take control.

00:08:28.744 --> 00:08:33.230
There were many problems between the British and the Governor of Bengal.

00:08:33.290 --> 00:08:38.042
Siraj Aldoula, who had taken Calcutta from the British.

00:08:38.102 --> 00:08:42.655
The tension led to the Battle of Placie in 1757.

00:08:42.715 --> 00:08:51.789
The British defeated Siraj Aldoula's army and then bribed their candidate, Mia Yafor, to become the Governor of Bengal.

00:08:51.849 --> 00:09:00.254
This victory in the Battle of Plasi led to the expanding of British rule in India, which lasted for almost 200 years.

00:09:00.314 --> 00:09:09.660
In 1775, there was a fight for power among the Peshwas, and the British East Indian Company was implicated.

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:17.166
This British interference in the affairs of the Maratas led to the first of three Anglo-Maratas wars.

00:09:17.226 --> 00:09:22.738
In the Third War, in 1818, the British defeated the Maratas.

00:09:22.798 --> 00:09:28.326
Now British rule was even stronger and they influenced daily life in India.

00:09:28.386 --> 00:09:35.968
Because the British needed more money, taxes and territories to support their large armies, they continued to fight.

00:09:36.028 --> 00:09:40.558
They conquered territory after territory and their empire grew.

00:09:40.618 --> 00:09:49.316
The Indian people soon got tired of British rulers who had no respect for their religions and who tried to change some of their traditions.

00:09:49.376 --> 00:10:06.778
In 1857, the British officers went one step too far by ordering Indian soldiers to load ammunition that had cow and pig fat on it, even though they knew that cows are holy to Hindus and pigs are repulsive to Muslims.

00:10:06.838 --> 00:10:17.981
Many people condemned the East India Company for allowing this to happen, and it led to the Indian Mutiny, which took the lives of hundreds of thousands of soldiers.

00:10:18.041 --> 00:10:30.296
At this point, Queen Victoria finally realised that a trading company couldn't rule an empire and in 1858 she made India part of the British Empire.

00:10:30.356 --> 00:10:34.078
British rule and the struggle for freedom.

00:10:34.138 --> 00:10:44.558
In 1905, the British government adopted a divide and rule policy to stop Indians from uniting against British rule in India.

00:10:44.618 --> 00:10:52.360
They divided Bengal into West Bengal, mainly Hindu and East Bengal, mainly Muslim.

00:10:52.420 --> 00:11:03.305
Because if the Hindus and Muslims were united, they would have too much power and could challenge British control over India, the jewel in the crown of the British Empire.

00:11:03.365 --> 00:11:17.342
In 1915, Mahatma Gandhi became president of the Indian National Congress, also known as the Congress Party, founded in 1885 to fight against British rule in India.

00:11:17.402 --> 00:11:27.254
He led the freedom struggle and believed in Ahimsa, the practice of non-violence, and used this in the fight for independence.

00:11:27.314 --> 00:11:37.200
In 1919, the British government approved a law allowing them to silence the press and imprison political activists without a trial.

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:43.976
Things got worse when hundreds of innocent Indian civilians were massacred at political meetings.

00:11:44.036 --> 00:11:50.446
Now the Indians hated the British more than ever and were even more determined to fight them.

00:11:50.506 --> 00:11:59.782
Gandhi's answer was to create the non-cooperation movement in 1920 to continue the struggle for Indian independence.

00:11:59.842 --> 00:12:06.044
This movement was one of Gandhi's first organized acts of civil disobedience.

00:12:06.104 --> 00:12:19.946
In this same year, the Indian National Congress reorganized itself and performed acts of civil disobedience like the Salt March in 1930 to protest against British taxes on salt.

00:12:20.006 --> 00:12:27.336
Gandhi decided to march 388 kilometers from his home to the Arabian Sea.

00:12:27.396 --> 00:12:30.027
As he marched, others joined him.

00:12:30.087 --> 00:12:35.018
By the 5th of April 1930, they were several thousand.

00:12:35.078 --> 00:12:37.739
Gandhi picked up salt from the ground.

00:12:37.799 --> 00:12:42.621
The others took salt too, but the British saw this act as a crime.

00:12:42.681 --> 00:12:47.552
The Indians were supposed to buy salt from them, not take it for free.

00:12:47.612 --> 00:12:50.336
They sent Gandhi to prison for this.

00:12:50.396 --> 00:12:58.450
In 1942, Gandhi started the Quit India Movement, which demanded the end of British rule over India.

00:12:58.510 --> 00:13:05.149
Marches, protests and freedom speeches followed, with many people arrested or killed.

00:13:05.209 --> 00:13:22.358
Fighting started between the Muslims and the Hindus, leading to the Indian Independence Act in 1947, by which the UK partitioned British India into two new independent dominions, India and Pakistan.

00:13:22.418 --> 00:13:25.750
In the end, Gandhi achieved his objective.

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:30.464
The British flag was lowered and India was independent.

00:13:30.524 --> 00:13:34.634
Unfortunately, India didn't live happily ever after.

00:13:34.694 --> 00:13:46.232
Four wars were fought between India and Pakistan from 1947 to 1999, mainly because of territorial conflicts over the Kashmir region.

00:13:46.292 --> 00:13:57.840
Tragically, on 30 January 1948, Mahatma Gandhi, the father of the Indian nation, was assassinated in New Delhi by a Hindu fanatic.

00:13:57.901 --> 00:14:06.314
ironic that the man who had dedicated his life to non-violent protest was himself defeated by a cruel act of violence.

00:14:06.374 --> 00:14:19.763
CHAPTER 3 THE PEOPLE OF INDIA Imagine approximately 1.35 billion people living in a country only three times bigger than the USA.

00:14:19.823 --> 00:14:24.086
It's not surprising that India is so overpopulated.

00:14:24.146 --> 00:14:27.739
It has the second largest population in the world.

00:14:27.799 --> 00:14:35.017
More than 80% of the population is Hindu, so you'd expect Hinduism to be the official religion.

00:14:35.078 --> 00:14:39.102
But in fact, India doesn't have an official religion.

00:14:39.162 --> 00:14:48.390
Hinduism, Islam, Buddhism, Sikhism and Genism are just some of the many religions followed by the people.

00:14:48.450 --> 00:14:56.952
India is also the world's largest democracy, and the slogan, one man, one vote, is taken very seriously.

00:14:57.012 --> 00:15:05.916
Everybody has the right to vote, even if they live deep in a forest, like Bharat Das Darshandas, an elderly temple priest.

00:15:05.976 --> 00:15:11.470
He is the only voter in the remote gear forest in the state of Gujarat.

00:15:11.530 --> 00:15:17.206
Two policemen travel through the lion-infested forest to get his vote.

00:15:17.266 --> 00:15:19.502
the caste system.

00:15:19.562 --> 00:15:29.480
For more than 3,000 years, India has had a caste system which most people believe came from Brahma, the Hindu god of creation.

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:37.658
This system divides the population into four main categories, according to social importance and profession.

00:15:37.718 --> 00:15:43.236
Brahmins, Kshatrias, Vaishas and Shudras.

00:15:43.296 --> 00:15:51.184
The delits, also called untouchables, weren't even part of the caste system because they were so low.

00:15:51.244 --> 00:15:57.578
Each caste is represented by a different part of the body of the Brahma statue.

00:15:57.638 --> 00:16:03.923
Yauhalal Nehru, the first Prime Minister of India, came from the Brahman caste.

00:16:03.983 --> 00:16:07.728
This is the highest caste for priests and teachers.

00:16:07.788 --> 00:16:14.372
Satata Gattama, the Buddha, was a Kshatriya, the second highest caste.

00:16:14.432 --> 00:16:19.094
Kshatriyas were warriors and rulers of towns and cities.

00:16:19.154 --> 00:16:28.148
Mahatma Gandhi was born to a family of Vaisas, the caste which were mainly farmers, merchants and traders.

00:16:28.208 --> 00:16:35.544
Chandra Gupta I, ruler of the Gupta dynasty, was a Shudra from the lowest caste.

00:16:35.604 --> 00:16:40.276
Shudras were labourers without any social and economic rights.

00:16:40.336 --> 00:16:46.296
However, Chandra Gupta I worked hard and became a Kshatriya.

00:16:46.356 --> 00:16:54.225
Ramnat Kovind was born a delit or untouchable, the people who cleaned the streets and the toilets.

00:16:54.285 --> 00:16:59.977
He came from a poor family and grew up in a poor hut with six brothers and sisters.

00:17:00.037 --> 00:17:05.548
He didn't even have a bicycle, so he walked eight kilometers to school every day.

00:17:05.608 --> 00:17:09.640
But believe it or not, he became president of India in 2017.

00:17:09.700 --> 00:17:13.564
Brahmins, priests and teachers.

00:17:13.625 --> 00:17:19.871
chaturias, warriors and rulers.

00:17:19.931 --> 00:17:25.375
Vaishas, farmers, traders and merchants.

00:17:25.435 --> 00:17:35.140
Shudras, labourers, delits, outside the caste system, street sweepers and toilet cleaners.

00:17:35.201 --> 00:17:50.442
The caste system remained unchanged for centuries, trapping people into a classification system from which it was very difficult to escape You were born into a caste and could only marry a person of the same caste.

00:17:50.502 --> 00:17:54.924
It was very difficult to move to a higher or lower caste.

00:17:54.984 --> 00:18:04.400
In rural communities, the upper and lower castes usually lived in segregated colonies and they didn't drink from the same springs.

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:09.402
Brahmins would not accept food or drink from sudras.

00:18:09.462 --> 00:18:16.799
Today the caste system still exists, mainly in rural areas, but it has much less influence.

00:18:16.859 --> 00:18:22.564
In the cities, different castes live side by side, and even in to marry.

00:18:22.625 --> 00:18:31.934
Some delits and low caste Indians have advanced to occupy important positions in the country, like Ramnatt Covind.

00:18:31.994 --> 00:18:42.216
Widows It might be hard to imagine, but India has another group of people who are treated even worse than the untouchables.

00:18:42.276 --> 00:18:43.718
Widows.

00:18:43.778 --> 00:18:50.924
There are approximately 55 million widows in India and their lives are very difficult.

00:18:50.984 --> 00:18:58.566
In the past, when a married man died in India, his widow used to kill herself on his funeral fire.

00:18:58.626 --> 00:19:06.712
Now widows are not expected to do this, but they are forced to mourn their dead husbands for the rest of their lives.

00:19:06.772 --> 00:19:11.673
They are rejected from society and people think they bring bad luck.

00:19:11.733 --> 00:19:22.645
Many widows are thrown out of their homes by their husbands' families, who consider them a financial dependent, or by their children who see them as a burden.

00:19:22.705 --> 00:19:27.877
They can't marry again or go to weddings, even of their own children.

00:19:27.937 --> 00:19:33.612
They must wear white, because in Hinduism, white is the colour of mourning.

00:19:33.672 --> 00:19:46.689
Even worse, widows are not allowed to work and provide for themselves, so they are forced to live in poverty, singing prayers in temples or begging for food and a roof over their head.

00:19:46.749 --> 00:19:56.392
Considered useless without a husband, thousands of them escaped to Brindavana, known as the city of widows in the north of India.

00:19:56.452 --> 00:20:03.748
This is home to more than 20,000 widows who depend on charity and are simply waiting to die.

00:20:03.808 --> 00:20:17.296
Some organisations are trying to help the widows of Brindavanan and give them some money every month, but as many can't read or write, they can't even manage to go to the bank to take their money out.

00:20:17.356 --> 00:20:25.028
Clearly, there is a long way to go to change the attitude to this vulnerable and neglected section of Indian society.

00:20:25.088 --> 00:20:26.338
4.

00:20:26.398 --> 00:20:29.750
Religion, customs and language.

00:20:29.810 --> 00:20:45.256
Religions There are four main religions in India, Hinduism, 80%, Islam, 14%, Christianity, 2% and Sikhism, 2%.

00:20:45.317 --> 00:20:52.529
Buddhism and Genism are also important and are followed by 1% of the population.

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:58.754
Hinduism To me, Hinduism is more a way of life than a religion.

00:20:58.814 --> 00:21:06.638
It developed from the religion of the Aryans who arrived in India around 1500 B.C.

00:21:06.698 --> 00:21:11.910
Like all Hindus, I believe in one God named Brahman.

00:21:11.970 --> 00:21:16.692
Many people think all Hindus are vegetarian, but that's not true.

00:21:16.752 --> 00:21:21.644
We are allowed to eat meat, although I prefer to be a vegetarian.

00:21:21.704 --> 00:21:30.859
Islam I come to you in the name of Allah, the one and only God of the Muslims, to teach Islamic law.

00:21:30.919 --> 00:21:36.681
In Islam, we don't believe in the caste system, we have our own social classes.

00:21:36.741 --> 00:21:43.777
We live according to the laws of the Quran, which prohibit us from eating pork.

00:21:43.837 --> 00:21:50.213
I am a Roman Catholic, one of many Christian denominations in India.

00:21:50.273 --> 00:21:58.056
I believe in one God and that every human being is his child and a brother or sister of Jesus Christ.

00:21:58.116 --> 00:22:01.416
I want to break down the caste system.

00:22:01.476 --> 00:22:10.680
Sikhism I am a follower of the Sikh religion which was founded by Guru Nanak in the late 15th century.

00:22:10.740 --> 00:22:17.661
We believe in one God and we believe you don't have to pray or go to a priest to connect with him.

00:22:17.721 --> 00:22:21.092
In the Sikh religion everyone is equal.

00:22:21.152 --> 00:22:25.493
We don't have social classes and don't follow the caste system.

00:22:25.553 --> 00:22:30.196
When it comes to food we can choose to eat meat or not.

00:22:30.256 --> 00:22:31.999
Genism.

00:22:32.059 --> 00:22:37.294
If you can't live without your mobile phone, you wouldn't make a very good gen.

00:22:37.354 --> 00:22:42.568
We don't believe in material things or showing violence to any living being.

00:22:42.628 --> 00:22:44.520
We have got many gods.

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:48.434
We don't believe in the caste system and we're vegetarian.

00:22:48.494 --> 00:22:57.557
Actually, I don't even eat onions or potatoes, because when you pull them up from the earth, it damages tiny organisms in there.

00:22:57.617 --> 00:22:59.244
Buddhism.

00:22:59.304 --> 00:23:03.825
Prince Siddhartha Gattama, the Buddha, is my hero.

00:23:03.885 --> 00:23:10.138
He became enlightened and founded Buddhism over two and a half thousand years ago.

00:23:10.198 --> 00:23:16.340
That is my mission, to have my eyes opened to see how the world really works.

00:23:16.400 --> 00:23:23.284
I go to a special place called an ashram to do yoga and meditation and to teach religion.

00:23:23.344 --> 00:23:27.476
I'm vegetarian, but it's not a rule among Buddhists.

00:23:27.536 --> 00:23:34.318
I don't believe in the caste system and I don't believe in the existence of just one God.

00:23:34.378 --> 00:23:45.398
Customs and Beliefs Holy cow is a way of showing surprise in English, but did you know that cows really are holy to Hindus?

00:23:45.459 --> 00:23:49.296
In Hinduism, the mother figure is like a goddess.

00:23:49.356 --> 00:23:55.078
Since cows give milk and have a gentle nature, they are associated with mothers.

00:23:55.138 --> 00:23:59.190
They are considered holy and Hindus idolise them.

00:23:59.250 --> 00:24:06.122
In India, there are more than 3,000 institutions which look after old or sick cows.

00:24:06.182 --> 00:24:14.226
Symbolism of the Snake You might think snakes are scary and dangerous, but not in India.

00:24:14.286 --> 00:24:23.274
There the snake is holy, a symbol of rebirth, death and mortality, because it sheds its skin and is reborn.

00:24:23.334 --> 00:24:28.768
All around India you can see cobras made from wood or stone.

00:24:28.828 --> 00:24:35.572
Some Indians believe that if you accidentally kill a cobra, it must be cremated like a human.

00:24:35.632 --> 00:24:46.458
In addition, both Hindus and Buddhists believe in the holiness of a serpent God called naga, and every year they have a procession in its honour.

00:24:46.518 --> 00:24:53.322
Hindus idolise snakes in temples, offering them milk, incense and prayers.

00:24:53.382 --> 00:25:04.387
Language Hindi and English are the official languages of India, although Sanskrit is spoken by Hindus, Buddhists and gens.

00:25:04.447 --> 00:25:14.260
At the time of British rule in India, the country was divided into many states with their own dialects and 22 different languages were spoken.

00:25:14.320 --> 00:25:20.184
English was a common language for people of all the different areas to communicate with each other.

00:25:20.244 --> 00:25:25.228
It remained the language of communication after the British left the country.

00:25:25.288 --> 00:25:34.010
English is widely spoken in the country today and ironically India is the largest English speaking nation in the world.

00:25:34.070 --> 00:25:40.910
Most colleges and universities use English for teaching, and it has become a language of status.

00:25:40.971 --> 00:25:46.666
If you want to study at university and get a good job, you must know English.

00:25:46.726 --> 00:25:51.078
However, don't assume that all Indians speak English fluently.

00:25:51.138 --> 00:25:52.200
They don't.

00:25:52.260 --> 00:25:57.390
Also, understanding their accent can sometimes be quite a challenge.

00:25:57.450 --> 00:26:09.166
The elephant holds a special place in Indian culture, particularly in the ancient temples of Kerala, southwestern India, which are home to hundreds of captive elephants.

00:26:09.226 --> 00:26:19.691
Hindus believe elephants are holy animals, the reincarnation of Lord Ganesh, a god with an elephant head who rides on a tiny mouse.

00:26:19.751 --> 00:26:32.670
Many tourists come to Kerala to admire the elephants, particularly during festival season, December to May, when the animals are decorated with ornaments and exhibited through the streets.

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:45.953
It's an extravagant and colourful spectacle, but many people think it's cruel as the elephants are chained around their legs and sometimes abused by their keepers, or mahouts.

00:26:46.014 --> 00:26:52.904
Lifestyle Indian culture is full of unique traditions and customs.

00:26:52.964 --> 00:26:57.048
One of these practices is the habit of being late.

00:26:57.108 --> 00:27:00.330
In India, time has little meaning.

00:27:00.390 --> 00:27:08.808
People say the only time Indians aren't late is for cricket matches and that's probably because the umpires aren't Indians.

00:27:08.868 --> 00:27:12.344
Indians have their own particular way of dressing.

00:27:12.404 --> 00:27:19.540
women often wear a traditional dress called as sari, which is wrapped around the body and over the shoulder.

00:27:19.600 --> 00:27:23.311
Men in villages still wear traditional clothes.

00:27:23.371 --> 00:27:30.103
A kurda is a shirt that reaches the knees and is worn over a skirt-like garment.

00:27:30.163 --> 00:27:39.952
The salwa kamis, tapered trousers and a long tunic is another type of dress that has become very popular with the younger generation.

00:27:40.012 --> 00:27:43.759
Traditional values are very important to Indians.

00:27:43.819 --> 00:27:52.981
Both in the past and present, an Indian's family name is based on his caste, place of origin or family occupation.

00:27:53.041 --> 00:28:02.953
Men are still considered the head of the family, but today women work too and contribute significantly to the family's financial situation.

00:28:03.013 --> 00:28:11.806
Chapter 5 Geography Great things often happen by accident, and Barat is one of them.

00:28:11.866 --> 00:28:18.618
Barat is the official name of the Republic of India, the seventh biggest country in the world.

00:28:18.678 --> 00:28:29.362
Around 50 or 60 million years ago, this enormous peninsula crashed into Asia, forming the Himalayas and Hindu Kush mountains.

00:28:29.422 --> 00:28:42.748
The Himalayas in the north form the world's highest mountains, Many of India's rarest animals and plants live there, and if you're very lucky, you may even see a snow leopard.

00:28:42.808 --> 00:28:53.581
The Indian Peninsula is surrounded by the Bay of Bengal in the southeast, the Arabian Sea in the southwest and the Indian Ocean in the south.

00:28:53.641 --> 00:29:04.318
It's bordered by Bhutan, China and Nepal in the northeast, Myanmar and Bangladesh in the east, and Pakistan in the west.

00:29:04.378 --> 00:29:13.358
One of the things that make India so unique is its strategic location, halfway between East and West Asia.

00:29:13.418 --> 00:29:21.624
From India, Africa is easily accessible via the Indian Ocean and Europe via the Suez Canal.

00:29:21.684 --> 00:29:29.773
The Strait of Malacca is one of the most important shipping routes between the Indian Ocean and the Pacific Ocean.

00:29:29.833 --> 00:29:37.199
Before 1911, the capital of India was Kolkata, formerly known as Kolkata.

00:29:37.259 --> 00:29:53.608
But on the 12th of December 1911, the British King George VI, who was also Emperor of India, announced that the capital should be in Delhi instead, as this was the political and financial centre of the country.

00:29:53.668 --> 00:29:58.640
The new capital, New Delhi, was inaugurated in 1931.

00:29:58.700 --> 00:30:05.875
Today, New Delhi has a population of almost 19 million people.

00:30:05.936 --> 00:30:11.363
Climate There's a variety of terrain in India.

00:30:11.423 --> 00:30:15.734
In the tar desert, in the west, the weather is hot and dry.

00:30:15.794 --> 00:30:19.616
But if you travel to the northeast, you'll find jungles.

00:30:19.676 --> 00:30:23.957
In northern India, there's an area called the Ganges Plain.

00:30:24.017 --> 00:30:32.069
It's very fertile because water from natural springs runs down the Himalayas, taking fertile sediments with it.

00:30:32.129 --> 00:30:38.444
This is very important for growing crops, since half the country depends on agriculture.

00:30:38.504 --> 00:30:44.402
When there are floods in the Ganges Plain, the farmers use the water for irrigation.

00:30:44.462 --> 00:30:50.626
Floods are common in India because it has so many rivers and is a peninsula.

00:30:50.686 --> 00:31:01.214
There are also the monsoons which occur from June to September when the direction of the wind changes causing a lot of heavy rain to fall.

00:31:01.274 --> 00:31:09.414
Wildlife In some parts of India, don't be surprised if you see an elephant having a massage.

00:31:09.474 --> 00:31:21.876
In Guru Vaya Elephant Sanctuary, there's a spa exclusively for elephants where they get royal treatment, luxurious baths, relaxing massages and a special diet.

00:31:21.936 --> 00:31:27.868
The elephants here are much more fortunate than those who work for the tourist industry.

00:31:27.928 --> 00:31:33.268
India is home to a wide range of animals thanks to its varied climate.

00:31:33.328 --> 00:31:39.080
It's the only country in the world where you can find both lions and tigers.

00:31:39.140 --> 00:31:46.664
On the coast of the Bay of Bengal lies the Sundarbans, the world's biggest mangrove forest.

00:31:46.724 --> 00:31:55.313
Here you can find tigers swimming in the same rivers as dolphins, sea turtles, sharks and saltwater crocodiles.

00:31:55.373 --> 00:32:03.215
Sadly this area is being destroyed by man who hunts animals and cuts down trees for firewood.

00:32:03.275 --> 00:32:11.193
The Ganges River The Ganges got its name from the Hindu goddess Ganges.

00:32:11.253 --> 00:32:26.310
This 2500km long river plays a very important role in the lifestyle of the people who live on its banks They grow crops along the river because the earth is very fertile and they use its water for irrigation.

00:32:26.370 --> 00:32:32.802
Varanasi, the holiest city in Hinduism, is located on the banks of the Ganges.

00:32:32.862 --> 00:32:40.145
People throw the ashes of their dead relatives into the river as they believe it sends the ashes to heaven.

00:32:40.206 --> 00:32:52.850
The Ganges is the fifth most contaminated river in the world, but that doesn't stop it from being India's most important and holy river to more than one billion Indians.

00:32:52.910 --> 00:32:59.630
Locals bathe in the Ganges, drink from it and perform purification rituals in it.

00:32:59.690 --> 00:33:05.220
At the Kumbhamella festival, millions of people come to wash away their sins.

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:12.883
The festival takes place in Alabad, a city in northern India where three important rivers meet.

00:33:12.943 --> 00:33:24.282
Crowds of people stand on the riverbank at Sangam, the meeting point of these three rivers, reciting Hindu scriptures as they wait for their turn to enter the cold water.

00:33:24.342 --> 00:33:28.359
Religious leaders from Hindu monasteries lead the ceremony.

00:33:28.419 --> 00:33:33.662
They throw flowers at the people in the water shouting, long live the Ganges!

00:33:33.722 --> 00:33:45.759
Chapter 6 Famous Places and Attractions The Taj Mahal The Taj Mahal in Agra is one of the seven wonders of the modern world.

00:33:45.819 --> 00:33:55.852
You may think $827 million is a lot to spend on someone you love, but Emperor Shah Jahan didn't think twice.

00:33:55.912 --> 00:34:07.238
In the 17th century, he built the Taj Mahal as a symbol of love in memory of his third and favorite wife, the Persian princess Mumtaz Mahal.

00:34:07.298 --> 00:34:16.456
Shah loved Mumtaz with all his heart, and when she died, while giving birth to their 14th child, he was heartbroken.

00:34:16.517 --> 00:34:19.207
He wanted to do something to honour her.

00:34:19.267 --> 00:34:27.179
So he designed and ordered the building of the world's most beautiful tomb on the banks of the Yamuna River.

00:34:27.239 --> 00:34:38.831
It took 20,000 workers, almost 22 years to build this spectacular mausoleum from white marble, with floral decorations made from precious stones.

00:34:38.891 --> 00:34:45.553
Every year, around 6 million people visit the Taj Mahal and admire its splendour.

00:34:45.613 --> 00:34:47.534
Mumbai.

00:34:47.595 --> 00:34:51.807
Mumbai is the most overpopulated city in India.

00:34:51.867 --> 00:34:55.478
It's busy, crowded and unpredictable.

00:34:55.538 --> 00:35:06.584
Don't be surprised to see a poor hut next to a multi-million dollar home or a national park in the middle of the city with wild animals walking around freely.

00:35:06.644 --> 00:35:10.306
Almost 14 million people live in Mumbai.

00:35:10.366 --> 00:35:15.484
That's more than twice the number of inhabitants per square kilometre in New York.

00:35:15.544 --> 00:35:24.222
What's more, by 2020, it's estimated that Mumbai will have a population of 27 million people.

00:35:24.282 --> 00:35:30.637
Mumbai and New York have their similarities, but pace isn't one of them.

00:35:30.697 --> 00:35:36.849
If you're in a hurry, don't try to send a package to a foreign country from the Indian Post Office.

00:35:36.909 --> 00:35:46.931
A special person, called a package waller, puts your package in a cloth sack and seals it with a wax stamp, which takes over an hour.

00:35:46.991 --> 00:35:52.834
Riding a train in Mumbai during rush hour is an experience you'll never forget.

00:35:52.894 --> 00:35:59.549
The Indian Railway Company tries to fit 500 people into a train carriage meant for 188.

00:35:59.569 --> 00:36:03.392
The rest sit on the roof.

00:36:03.452 --> 00:36:09.766
Is that why around 3000 people die in train related accidents every year?

00:36:09.826 --> 00:36:14.636
Film City Who hasn't heard of Bollywood?

00:36:14.696 --> 00:36:17.351
The Hindi language film industry.

00:36:17.412 --> 00:36:25.367
The name comes from a combination of Bombay, now known as Mumbai, and Hollywood, the American equivalent.

00:36:25.427 --> 00:36:31.891
Unlike Hollywood, Bollywood isn't an actual place, but Film City is.

00:36:31.952 --> 00:36:37.143
It's located in Mumbai and almost all Bollywood films are made there.

00:36:37.203 --> 00:36:48.766
At Film City, there are many different types of film locations, including lakes, gardens, villages, a temple, a prison, a court and a waterfall.

00:36:48.826 --> 00:36:52.776
You can take a guided tour and visit film sets.

00:36:52.836 --> 00:37:05.836
those who prefer to be in a Bollywood film and not just visit the set of one can spend time in a popular shopping and dining district where foreigners are often asked if they would like to be extras.

00:37:05.896 --> 00:37:15.785
Maybe 800 rupees, around 10 euros, isn't much for a day's work but the experience is certainly worth a lot more.

00:37:15.845 --> 00:37:24.638
Varanasi For some tourists, it's quite a shock to see the sight of unburned body parts floating on the Ganges.

00:37:24.698 --> 00:37:31.774
That's a sure sign that they're in Varanasi, the city of 23,000 temples.

00:37:31.834 --> 00:37:36.648
Almost two and a half million people visit Varanasi every year.

00:37:36.708 --> 00:37:42.292
Many of them are close to the end of their life and they come to bathe in the Holy River.

00:37:42.352 --> 00:37:48.955
In Hinduism and Buddhism, Nirvana is the highest state that someone can reach.

00:37:49.015 --> 00:37:54.310
It's a state of enlightenment, a place of perfect peace and happiness.

00:37:54.370 --> 00:38:04.851
Hindus believe that dying in Varanasi brings salvation and Nirvana, so they travel to the city as soon as they realize they are close to death.

00:38:04.912 --> 00:38:09.748
After death, they are purified in the Ganges and then cremated.

00:38:09.808 --> 00:38:13.952
Any unburned body parts are thrown into the river.

00:38:14.012 --> 00:38:19.255
Gats are places with steps that lead down to the banks of the river.

00:38:19.315 --> 00:38:28.922
In Varanasi, some Gats are for bathing or religious ceremonies, while others are only used for cremations, Chapter 7.

00:38:28.982 --> 00:38:39.177
Festivals Indians celebrate a multitude of festivals that contribute to the country's rich culture, traditions and spirituality.

00:38:39.237 --> 00:38:46.992
Some of the festivals are celebrated all around the country, while others are only celebrated in specific regions.

00:38:47.052 --> 00:38:53.027
Being a part of any of these festivals is a colourful experience.

00:38:53.088 --> 00:39:03.168
horrendous traffic jams and a spectacular fireworks display announced that Diwali, the Hindu festival of lights, has arrived.

00:39:03.228 --> 00:39:07.080
This is India's biggest and most important holiday.

00:39:07.140 --> 00:39:13.544
The name de valley means rows of lighted lamps, and it's easy to see why.

00:39:13.604 --> 00:39:26.244
The whole country is illuminated by lamps called DS, candles and fireworks as Hindus celebrate the triumph of light over dark and of good over evil.

00:39:26.304 --> 00:39:32.886
Hindus believe in one true God, Brahman, but they have many other gods too.

00:39:32.946 --> 00:39:38.561
One of the most important gods is Lord Rama, who had many adventures.

00:39:38.621 --> 00:39:46.226
In one of these, he killed the demon king Ravana, who had captured Rama's beloved wife Sita.

00:39:46.286 --> 00:39:56.128
The lamps that illuminate India during Diwali are to help Rama and Sita find their way home after the defeat of the demon.

00:39:56.188 --> 00:40:07.766
In Tharavy, a very poor area in Mumbai, 2,500 potter families spend days making DS before the festival starts.

00:40:07.826 --> 00:40:11.606
Men make them from clay and bake them in ovens.

00:40:11.666 --> 00:40:16.637
After that women paint them and children help to pack them for sale.

00:40:16.697 --> 00:40:20.118
Everybody plays a part in the festivities.

00:40:20.178 --> 00:40:27.363
Like Christmas, Diwali is a holiday where families come together to share sweets and gifts.

00:40:27.423 --> 00:40:35.718
It's celebrated for five days, and on each day, different prayers are recited and different rituals are performed.

00:40:35.778 --> 00:40:45.928
In certain regions of Rajasthan, women participate in a special ritual, dancing in a circle with a flaming pot on their heads.

00:40:45.988 --> 00:40:59.780
In Goa, people build big, scary statues of the legendary tyrant Narakasura, a cruel and unpopular leader who tried to kill one of their beloved gods, Lord Krishna.

00:40:59.840 --> 00:41:09.896
Lord Krishna cut off Narakasura's head, and in celebration of this, the huge statues are burnt on the night before Diwali.

00:41:09.956 --> 00:41:18.118
Durga Puja Durga Puja is both an arts festival and a religious celebration.

00:41:18.178 --> 00:41:24.974
It is celebrated mainly in West Bengal and Kolkata is the best place to experience it.

00:41:25.034 --> 00:41:38.012
The five day festival is in honor of the goddess Durga, the symbol of divine feminine energy and her victory over the evil buffalo demon Mahishasura.

00:41:38.072 --> 00:41:42.714
Kolkata's artisans spend a year preparing for the festival.

00:41:42.774 --> 00:41:49.416
They make special platforms and display ten armed statues of the goddess Durga on them.

00:41:49.476 --> 00:42:03.161
During the festival, people go from platform to platform to see these works of art, and on the last day, they join a procession to the Ganges River, where the statues are submerged in the water.

00:42:03.221 --> 00:42:07.642
Durga Puja has a number of religious rituals.

00:42:07.702 --> 00:42:19.328
In one of them a small banana plant is dressed in a sari, bazed in a nearby river and then used to attract Durga's energy and holy presence.

00:42:19.388 --> 00:42:29.395
Huli Huli, known as the festival of colours, celebrates the arrival of spring and the victory of good over evil.

00:42:29.455 --> 00:42:38.186
In most places, pulley is celebrated for two days, and if you don't mind getting wet and dirty, it can be a lot of fun.

00:42:38.246 --> 00:42:46.644
The first night, Holikadan, starts with a public fire, symbolising the burning of Holikah, the devil.

00:42:46.704 --> 00:42:50.956
People have parties and dance under water sprinklers.

00:42:51.016 --> 00:42:54.368
Rangwali huli starts the next morning.

00:42:54.428 --> 00:43:01.976
People put colour all over each other's faces and throw water on each other while saying, don't be offended.

00:43:02.036 --> 00:43:03.737
It's huli.

00:43:03.798 --> 00:43:13.767
Some of them first put coconut oil on their skin, which makes it easier to clean, so they won't look like a walking rainbow for a week after the festival.

00:43:13.827 --> 00:43:22.401
The British band Coldplay filmed the video clip of their song, Him for the Weekend, at the Holy Festival.

00:43:22.461 --> 00:43:23.311
8.

00:43:23.371 --> 00:43:35.852
Food Glorious Food India's multinational population and a significant role in the spice trade explain why Indian food explodes with flavour.

00:43:35.912 --> 00:43:53.566
A variety of factors have influenced Indian cooking, like the chili pepper brought from Mexico by the Portuguese, the chutney developed by British housewives and their Indian cooks, and new food ideas introduced by Indians returning from their travels.

00:43:53.626 --> 00:43:58.386
Many of the dishes are based on rice, grains and pulses.

00:43:58.446 --> 00:44:10.546
Although the food differs from region to region, you can be certain that everything is made with plenty of spices, like garam masala, a combination of dried spices.

00:44:10.606 --> 00:44:14.890
Chutney is an important ingredient in Indian dishes.

00:44:14.950 --> 00:44:21.676
Many of us have eaten sweet mango chutney, sour mint chutney or hot chili chutney.

00:44:21.736 --> 00:44:26.770
But you've probably never tried chapla, red ant chutney.

00:44:26.830 --> 00:44:37.836
A tribe from Bastar, in Chhattisgar, collects tons of stinging biting ants and their eggs, which are crushed, dried and ground into a powder.

00:44:37.896 --> 00:44:48.592
tomatoes, cilantro, garlic, ginger, chili, salt and sugar are added to make the hot spicy orange-coloured chutney.

00:44:48.652 --> 00:44:57.428
If you don't mind eating ants, chakra is very healthy because ants contain an acid with medicinal properties.

00:44:57.488 --> 00:44:59.789
exotic foods.

00:44:59.849 --> 00:45:06.352
If you think Indian food is only about spices, curries and vegetables, think again.

00:45:06.412 --> 00:45:11.225
In Assam, people eat a delicacy called eripolu.

00:45:11.285 --> 00:45:20.874
It's made from the pupa of silkworms and served with chorizo, a dish made from fermented bamboo shoots.

00:45:20.934 --> 00:45:27.502
Another exotic dish, sore potter, is a kind of stew that's eaten in Goa.

00:45:27.562 --> 00:45:32.835
It looks quite harmless, but it's made from a pig's internal organs.

00:45:32.896 --> 00:45:38.549
Dishes made from dried fish are another delicacy in many parts of India.

00:45:38.609 --> 00:45:43.382
Nakam takes the prize for being the smellyest of all the fish dishes.

00:45:43.442 --> 00:45:49.837
It's a curry made from dried fish mixed with vegetables and distilled ashes.

00:45:49.897 --> 00:45:51.628
Bon appetit!

00:45:51.688 --> 00:46:08.286
The tali Indian restaurants offer a multitude of dishes, so the tali, the Hindi word for a large plate, is the perfect solution for indecisive people, and it's popular in many regions of the country.

00:46:08.346 --> 00:46:14.109
It consists of a selection of curries, vegetables, sauces and yogurt.

00:46:14.169 --> 00:46:20.561
The food is served in small bowls on a round tray with a mountain of rice in the middle.

00:46:20.621 --> 00:46:26.966
It comes with chutney, pickles and poppodums, a thin round crispy bread.

00:46:27.026 --> 00:46:31.048
The dishes in the tali are eaten in a special order.

00:46:31.108 --> 00:46:39.320
For example, you start with the vegetables, followed by rice, mixed with a spicy lentil sauce called sambha.

00:46:39.380 --> 00:46:46.970
Next, the spiciest of the sauces is served, and then the rasam, a sauce made from tomatoes.

00:46:47.031 --> 00:46:51.092
You eat a small portion of rice with each of the sauces.

00:46:51.152 --> 00:46:57.746
By this time, your palate will be on fire, so you'll be ready for the yogurt mixed with rice.

00:46:57.806 --> 00:47:02.308
For dessert, the tali sometimes includes something sweet.

00:47:02.368 --> 00:47:07.046
Tali is good for your digestion, because it's a balanced meal.

00:47:07.106 --> 00:47:08.874
eating habits.

00:47:08.935 --> 00:47:15.098
It's not just the food in India that's unusual, but also the way it's eaten and served.

00:47:15.158 --> 00:47:22.375
In Europe, eating with your hands is not considered very polite, but in India, it's a tradition.

00:47:22.435 --> 00:47:29.070
In fact, when you eat with your hands, you enjoy your food more because it stimulates the senses.

00:47:29.130 --> 00:47:41.439
Indians believe that touching your food helps you feel the taste, texture and aroma, but you must only use your right hand because the left hand is considered unclean.

00:47:41.499 --> 00:47:47.771
In the south of India, it's a tradition to serve food on banana leaves instead of plates.

00:47:47.831 --> 00:47:55.423
Firstly, it's healthier because when you put hot food on a banana leaf, it releases nutrients that are good for you.

00:47:55.483 --> 00:47:58.964
Secondly, it tastes and smells better.

00:47:59.024 --> 00:48:01.985
Thirdly, it's environmentally friendly.

00:48:02.045 --> 00:48:06.306
But the best part is that there are no plates to wash at the end of the meal.

00:48:06.366 --> 00:48:08.787
Maybe we should all try this idea.

00:48:08.847 --> 00:48:10.308
CHAPTER 9.

00:48:10.368 --> 00:48:14.782
FAMOUS PEOPLE Mahatma Gandhi.

00:48:14.842 --> 00:48:25.890
Mohandas Karamchand Gandhi was known to his people as Mahatma, which means Great Soul, or Bapu, which means Father.

00:48:25.950 --> 00:48:31.584
Gandhi was born in Pobanda, India in 1869.

00:48:31.644 --> 00:48:40.236
He came from a well-respected family and his parents arranged for him to marry Kasturba Mahangi when he was 13.

00:48:40.296 --> 00:48:46.125
This may seem strange to you, but for an Indian boy it was expected.

00:48:46.186 --> 00:48:54.779
His parents wanted him to become a lawyer, so he studied law in London and then went to South Africa to find employment.

00:48:54.839 --> 00:49:03.482
While he was travelling on a train one day, Gandhi was asked to leave the first-class carriage even though he had a valid ticket.

00:49:03.542 --> 00:49:04.921
The reason?

00:49:04.981 --> 00:49:06.572
His skin colour.

00:49:06.632 --> 00:49:12.848
He was furious and this marked the start of his fight against racial discrimination.

00:49:12.908 --> 00:49:23.478
In 1894, Gandhi founded the Natale Indian Congress and protested against injustice to Indians in South Africa.

00:49:23.538 --> 00:49:31.241
Gandhi stayed in South Africa for 20 years and then returned to India, but things weren't better there.

00:49:31.301 --> 00:49:41.223
The British discriminated against the Indians and Gandhi refused to accept this, so he dedicated his life to fighting for equality.

00:49:41.283 --> 00:49:52.612
He organised non-violent civil disobedience campaigns in which people didn't go to work or eat, sat in the streets or boycotted the courts.

00:49:52.672 --> 00:50:00.352
These acts may seem small, but when thousands of people did them at the same time, it had a huge impact.

00:50:00.413 --> 00:50:16.385
Gandhi was arrested many times and imprisoned, but his many followers and worldwide support made sure that he didn't stay in prison for long, especially because the British were afraid of what might happen if he died in prison.

00:50:16.445 --> 00:50:23.452
Finally, Gandhi's efforts paid off and he won the battle for independence for the Indian people.

00:50:23.512 --> 00:50:35.972
Sadly, Gandhi the pacifist was shot and killed by a Hindu fanatic on his way to prayers in New Delhi on the 30th of January 1948.

00:50:36.032 --> 00:50:43.912
Mother Teresa won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1979.

00:50:43.972 --> 00:50:52.590
In 1985, she received the Medal of Freedom, the highest honour awarded by the President of the United States to a civilian.

00:50:52.650 --> 00:50:58.912
She devoted her life to charity work and was declared a saint in 2016.

00:50:58.972 --> 00:51:05.360
Mother Teresa was born in Scopia, Macedonia, in 1910.

00:51:05.420 --> 00:51:11.561
At the age of 18, she joined a group of nuns in Ireland, where she learned English.

00:51:11.621 --> 00:51:18.132
After training, she was sent to India, where she became a nun in 1931.

00:51:18.192 --> 00:51:23.603
In that year, she started teaching in a school for girls in Kolkata.

00:51:23.663 --> 00:51:29.908
At 36, she discovered her mission in life, to help the sick and the poor.

00:51:29.968 --> 00:51:34.564
She learned how to give medical care and started doing charity work.

00:51:34.624 --> 00:51:40.236
It wasn't easy, and there were times when she herself had to beg for food.

00:51:40.296 --> 00:51:54.827
In 1950, Mother Teresa founded The Missionaries of Charity, an organisation that cared for the poor, hungry, homeless, disabled, and anyone who was not accepted in society.

00:51:54.887 --> 00:52:01.537
She opened many charity houses around the world, but is best known for her work in India.

00:52:01.597 --> 00:52:09.020
Mother Teresa died at the age of 87, on the 5th of September 1997.

00:52:09.080 --> 00:52:17.773
At the time of her death, the missionaries of charity were running 610 missions in 123 countries.

00:52:17.833 --> 00:52:27.658
People say that if cricket were a religion, Sachin Tendulkar would be the god.

00:52:27.718 --> 00:52:34.994
The retired cricketer and captain is the greatest and most admired Indian cricketer of all time.

00:52:35.054 --> 00:52:40.042
No other player has ever scored so many runs in international cricket.

00:52:40.102 --> 00:52:55.563
Tendulkar received numerous awards for his notable achievements in sport and after his final match in 2013, he was awarded the Bharat Ratna, the highest award given by the Indian government to a civilian.

00:52:55.624 --> 00:53:00.139
No other sport person has ever received this award.

00:53:00.199 --> 00:53:04.562
Tendulkar was born in Mumbai in 1973.

00:53:04.623 --> 00:53:13.058
He started playing cricket when he was 11 and practiced for hours, showing his incredible batting skills while still at school.

00:53:13.118 --> 00:53:20.242
When he was 14, a famous Indian batsman called Sunil Gavaskar inspired him.

00:53:20.303 --> 00:53:29.277
Gavaskar made his international debut when he was 16 and 20 years later, Tendulkar broke Gavaskar's record.

00:53:29.338 --> 00:53:39.256
and Dilka was highly motivated and became a champion cricketer, representing Mumbai and India in cricket for almost 24 years.

00:53:39.317 --> 00:53:46.294
In 2011, he won the cricket world cup with his team, making India very proud.

00:53:46.354 --> 00:53:47.734
CHAPTER 10.

00:53:47.794 --> 00:53:50.614
SPORT cricket.

00:53:50.674 --> 00:53:56.598
There is no doubt that cricket is India's most popular sport, thanks to the British.

00:53:56.658 --> 00:54:01.362
In a cricket match, there are two teams with 11 players in each team.

00:54:01.422 --> 00:54:04.344
They take turns batting and fielding.

00:54:04.404 --> 00:54:09.408
The objective of the batting team is to score as many runs as possible.

00:54:09.468 --> 00:54:16.755
The fielding players run around the cricket field to catch the ball and stop the batsman from scoring runs.

00:54:16.816 --> 00:54:23.987
The winner is the team with the most runs in an innings, which is when every player in the team has batted.

00:54:24.047 --> 00:54:28.585
cricket became popular in India in the 17th century.

00:54:28.645 --> 00:54:36.180
During the British rule, the Maharaja, King of Patiala, played and supported cricket.

00:54:36.240 --> 00:54:44.978
He was captain of the Indian cricket team in 1911 and played in first class cricket matches between 1915 and 1937.

00:54:45.038 --> 00:54:57.159
The Maharaja Summer Residence, the Child Palace, had the world's highest cricket ground, which was 2,250 metres high with forests all around.

00:54:57.220 --> 00:55:08.536
India's national team won the Cricket World Cup in 1983 and 2011 as well as other important trophies.

00:55:08.596 --> 00:55:19.676
India also holds the world record for winning all the ICC International Cricket Council tournaments when MS Thune was the captain.

00:55:19.736 --> 00:55:31.172
Field Hockey Most Indian sports fans consider field hockey the national sport of India due to their excellent performances in past Olympics.

00:55:31.232 --> 00:55:35.324
In field hockey, there are 11 players in each team.

00:55:35.384 --> 00:55:44.726
The objective of the game is to score more goals than the opposing team by hitting the ball past the goalkeeper and across the goal line.

00:55:44.786 --> 00:55:49.197
Players aren't allowed to kick, hold or carry the ball.

00:55:49.257 --> 00:55:52.391
They use wooden hockey sticks instead.

00:55:52.451 --> 00:56:07.045
In the beginning, the Indian team was invincible, taking home six Olympic gold medals from 1928 to 1956 and two more in 1964 and 1980.

00:56:07.106 --> 00:56:16.542
In addition, India has won one silver and two bronze Olympic medals, more hockey medals than any other team in the world.

00:56:16.602 --> 00:56:23.715
They also won the 1975 World Cup tournament in Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia.

00:56:23.775 --> 00:56:30.444
But after 1980, they lost their supremacy, mainly because of a change in the rules.

00:56:30.504 --> 00:56:35.034
Until the early 1970s, hockey was played on grass.

00:56:35.094 --> 00:56:46.713
Then, in the mid-1970s, the International Hockey Federation decided to make it obligatory for all international hockey tournaments to be played on Astroturf.

00:56:46.773 --> 00:56:51.848
This change was implemented in the 1976 Montreal Olympics.

00:56:51.908 --> 00:56:53.628
And guess what happened?

00:56:53.688 --> 00:56:56.746
India didn't even win a bronze medal.

00:56:56.806 --> 00:57:04.054
there aren't many astroturf grounds in India because they are expensive to lay and difficult to play on.

00:57:04.114 --> 00:57:16.916
However, in western countries like Holland, Germany and Australia there are lots of astroturf grounds, so Europe and Australia have now become the leaders in field hockey.

00:57:16.976 --> 00:57:24.395
Today field hockey is much more popular than it used to be, both in India and other countries.

00:57:24.455 --> 00:57:28.844
Women also play field hockey and they are very good at it.

00:57:28.904 --> 00:57:41.520
According to the International Hockey Federation, the Indian women's hockey team is ranked 13th and it is won three gold medals consecutively in international competitions.

00:57:41.580 --> 00:57:43.251
Kabadi.

00:57:43.311 --> 00:57:48.784
Kabadi is a very exciting team sport that is popular in Indian villages.

00:57:48.844 --> 00:57:55.074
It dates back to ancient times and was played by princes to prove how strong they were.

00:57:55.134 --> 00:57:59.832
Kabadi is played on a small field divided into two halves.

00:57:59.892 --> 00:58:08.755
There are two teams of seven players and each team takes turns sending a raider and attacking player into the other half.

00:58:08.815 --> 00:58:17.208
The raider has to eliminate his opponents and then return to his half of the field without being stopped by the defenders.

00:58:17.268 --> 00:58:24.830
At the same time, he has to hold his breath and chant Kabadi Kabadi Kabadi the whole time.

00:58:24.890 --> 00:58:28.791
the team that eliminates more opponents wins.

00:58:28.851 --> 00:58:35.454
The founder of Buddhism, Prince Siddhata Gattama, played Kabadi with his friends.

00:58:35.514 --> 00:58:39.626
The game requires both intelligence and physical strength.

00:58:39.687 --> 00:58:46.144
You also have to be in good shape and be able to anticipate what the opponent is going to do.

00:58:46.204 --> 00:58:55.260
India has been very successful at this sport, winning all the Kabadi World Cup tournaments and gold medals in all the Asian games.

00:58:55.320 --> 00:58:58.314
CHAPTER 11 Films.

00:58:58.374 --> 00:59:01.081
More Fact Than Fiction.

00:59:01.141 --> 00:59:08.982
In the past, Indian films were mainly the Bollywood romantic musicals of the Hindi language film industry.

00:59:09.042 --> 00:59:24.512
But more recently, English films about India expose the western world to some of the country's more serious problems, like poor sanitation, poverty, pollution, child marriage and the terrible treatment of widows.

00:59:24.572 --> 00:59:37.002
Many of these films are based on true stories and paint a realistic picture of daily life, projecting images that remain in the audience's mind even after they leave the cinema.

00:59:37.062 --> 00:59:43.062
These films show facts that cannot be ignored.

00:59:43.122 --> 00:59:59.769
The inspiration for Toilet, a love story, 2017, came from the true story of Anita Nari, a poor Indian woman who discovered after she got married that her new house didn't have a toilet.

00:59:59.829 --> 01:00:10.952
This may seem trivial to you, but to Indian women who are in danger of being sexually assaulted if they relieve themselves outside, it's a serious problem.

01:00:11.012 --> 01:00:20.480
For this reason, many women go out in groups when they need to go to the toilet and wait until it gets dark so that nobody will see them.

01:00:20.540 --> 01:00:30.066
In the film, Anita almost divorces her husband, but in the end, he builds her a toilet in their home and their marriage is saved.

01:00:30.127 --> 01:00:40.812
People relieving themselves in public places is a terrible problem in India, and according to UNICEF, more than half a million people still do that.

01:00:40.872 --> 01:00:45.552
The government has initiated a campaign to try and solve this problem.

01:00:45.612 --> 01:00:54.575
A new mobile phone app tells people where to find the nearest toilet, and thousands of toilets have been installed around the country.

01:00:54.635 --> 01:00:59.416
But unfortunately, many of them don't actually work.

01:00:59.476 --> 01:01:19.256
Water Deeper Mehta's Water 2005 examines the social restrictions imposed on widows and exposes an ancient Hindu law that a widow must either marry her dead husband's brother or kill herself on her husband's funeral fire.

01:01:19.316 --> 01:01:23.128
This film caused a lot of controversy in India.

01:01:23.188 --> 01:01:35.254
On more than one occasion, the film sets were destroyed and deeper's life was threatened, so the Indo-Canadian director moved to the filming from India to Sri Lanka.

01:01:35.313 --> 01:01:45.817
Water is set in 1938, at a time when child marriage was common in India and even young child widows were discriminated against.

01:01:45.877 --> 01:01:53.164
It centres on the lives of Chewya, a little Indian girl who is widowed at the age of eight.

01:01:53.224 --> 01:02:02.608
After the death of her 40-year-old husband, Chuyah is sent to an ashram where her hair is cut off and she has to wear white.

01:02:02.668 --> 01:02:13.692
She believes that her mother will come and take her home, but in reality she is destined to remain in the ashram forever because she is now a widow.

01:02:13.752 --> 01:02:32.486
The award-winning film Lion, 2016, is based on the true story of Saru Brieli, a lost little boy who travels 1,600 kilometres alone by train to Kolkata, trying to find his big brother.

01:02:32.546 --> 01:02:40.010
Saru is put in an orphanage and finally adopted by an Australian couple who live in Tasmania.

01:02:40.070 --> 01:02:49.753
Every year, more than 80,000 children go missing in India, and over 11 million children spend their lives on the streets.

01:02:49.813 --> 01:03:01.498
Saru is one of them, and we see his struggle to survive as a street child, sleeping in train stations on a cardboard box and eating food from rubbish bins.

01:03:01.558 --> 01:03:09.444
As an adult, he lives a good life with his adopted family, but is tormented by memories of his childhood.

01:03:09.504 --> 01:03:15.324
After 25 years, Saru decides to try and find his biological mother.

01:03:15.384 --> 01:03:27.598
With the help of Google satellite maps and faint memories of the night he lost his brother, he struggles to try and locate the village where he was born and the family he left behind.

01:03:27.658 --> 01:03:39.172
Slum Dog Millionaire Slumdog Millionaire 2008 is based on the life of a real person named Sushil Kumar.

01:03:39.232 --> 01:03:45.976
The word slumdog refers to someone who lives in a slum, a very poor area of the town.

01:03:46.036 --> 01:04:03.106
Sushil 26 came from a very poor family that didn't even have money to buy a TV set, but his life changed forever when he won $1 million on the Indian version of the TV show who wants to be a millionaire.

01:04:03.166 --> 01:04:10.941
Through a series of flashbacks, Slumdog Millionaire shows the events that led Sushil to win the competition.

01:04:11.001 --> 01:04:19.536
We see the terrible living conditions and poor sanitation in Dharavi, a famous slum in contemporary Mumbai.

01:04:19.596 --> 01:04:26.739
For six children, two of whom came from the slums, acting in this film gave them a chance for a new life.

01:04:26.800 --> 01:04:38.605
Director Danny Boyle arranged for them to be educated after the filming and made sure that they attended the Oscars in 2009, where the film won several awards.

01:04:38.665 --> 01:04:42.641
CHAPTER 12 India today.

01:04:42.702 --> 01:04:49.156
Yauhalal Nehru, India's first Prime Minister, had high hopes for his country.

01:04:49.216 --> 01:05:01.054
The night before India was declared a republic, Nehru expressed his feelings of triumph, but warned the Indian people that their new freedom was going to bring responsibility.

01:05:01.114 --> 01:05:06.818
He warned them not to rest in the future, but to keep trying to make things better.

01:05:06.879 --> 01:05:18.547
Today India has the fastest growing economy in the world, and it is predicted that by 2050 it will have the second largest economy after China.

01:05:18.608 --> 01:05:27.854
The country is indisputably an emerging superpower, but the road to achieving Nehru's hopes for the future is long.

01:05:27.914 --> 01:05:33.528
Despite India's growing power, many obstacles still need to be dealt with.

01:05:33.588 --> 01:05:39.884
poverty, lack of sanitation, road accidents and pollution are everywhere.

01:05:39.944 --> 01:05:48.441
The government is taking measures to deal with these issues and the progress is significant, but it isn't happening fast enough.

01:05:48.501 --> 01:05:55.134
The Obstacles India has the world's largest poor population.

01:05:55.194 --> 01:06:05.647
Between 1993 and 2011, the number of poor people dropped 25%, but there are still around 270 million people living below the poverty line.

01:06:05.707 --> 01:06:17.250
Around 45% of these people can't read or write, which leads to poverty because they can't find jobs easily.

01:06:17.310 --> 01:06:19.882
The situation is improving though.

01:06:19.942 --> 01:06:26.575
Between 2001 and 2011, the number of illiterate people was reduced by 31 million.

01:06:26.635 --> 01:06:38.350
With regard to sanitation, 80% of the poor live in rural areas and have little access to basic services.

01:06:38.410 --> 01:06:48.750
Only 21% have proper toilets, 61% have electricity and only 6% have access to tap water.

01:06:48.810 --> 01:06:55.297
As a result, sanitation is poor and diseases spread easily and quickly.

01:06:55.358 --> 01:06:58.391
The good news is that things are changing.

01:06:58.451 --> 01:07:06.514
Today the average life expectancy is 69 whereas in 1960 it was 41.

01:07:06.574 --> 01:07:11.496
So we can see that India has made a lot of progress in this area.

01:07:11.556 --> 01:07:18.896
Another problem in India is the frequency of traffic accidents which is amongst the highest in the world.

01:07:18.956 --> 01:07:29.022
According to a government agency report, more than 135,000 people in India die in traffic accidents every year.

01:07:29.082 --> 01:07:34.876
One cause of accidents is drivers speeding while talking on their mobile phones.

01:07:34.936 --> 01:07:47.948
A group of Indian researchers have developed a low-cost device which prevents drivers from receiving or making mobile phone calls while driving, but allows other passengers to call.

01:07:48.009 --> 01:07:57.886
Other causes of accidents are driving under the influence of alcohol, not using seatbelts and bike riders not using helmets.

01:07:57.946 --> 01:08:03.447
Overcrowded roads in bad condition don't help the situation either.

01:08:03.508 --> 01:08:09.980
A visit to Agra will confirm how serious India's air pollution problem is.

01:08:10.040 --> 01:08:17.350
The once white marble exterior of the magnificent Taj Mahal is gradually turning yellow.

01:08:17.410 --> 01:08:26.764
In New Delhi, a 15-metre high mountain of rubbish supports the claim that the capital is one of the world's most polluted cities.

01:08:26.823 --> 01:08:38.722
There aren't enough disposal areas for all of India's rubbish, and the few existing ones can't possibly hold any more, so people burn waste outside their homes.

01:08:38.782 --> 01:08:45.543
This results in a blanket of smoke over many of India's villages, towns and cities.

01:08:45.603 --> 01:08:54.489
In addition, rubbish is thrown from passing trains and the waste from train toilets is released directly onto the tracks.

01:08:54.550 --> 01:08:59.940
Rubbish is everywhere, even in the sacred Ganges River.

01:09:00.000 --> 01:09:03.297
How is the government dealing with this problem?

01:09:03.358 --> 01:09:12.356
Firstly, they are paying people to sort the rubbish into categories to be recycled, redistributed or disposed of.

01:09:12.416 --> 01:09:19.191
People receive a salary and keep any additional money made from selling the things they find in the rubbish.

01:09:19.251 --> 01:09:26.285
This new measure reduces the rubbish significantly, helping the environment and reducing poverty.

01:09:26.345 --> 01:09:38.258
Secondly, new rules for waste disposal were introduced in 2016 to help the problem, but they haven't been very effective because they aren't enforced.

01:09:38.319 --> 01:09:47.062
Cheap Labor in India Have you ever wondered why so many companies outsource their call centres to India?

01:09:47.122 --> 01:09:54.354
It's because India has a high number of English speakers who are ready to work hard for a low salary.

01:09:54.414 --> 01:09:57.506
But they don't only work in call centres.

01:09:57.566 --> 01:10:08.942
When you buy something on Amazon or book a ticket on Virgin, a lot of the computer processing work is done by people in India as this is more lucrative.

01:10:09.001 --> 01:10:17.780
Tomorrow's India By 2087, India will have the world's largest population.

01:10:17.840 --> 01:10:31.243
Today, half of this population consists of young people under the age of 25, many coming from underdeveloped places in the north of the country without education or qualifications.

01:10:31.303 --> 01:10:38.169
However, these are India's future leaders, so the government would like to alter the situation.

01:10:38.229 --> 01:10:48.406
They created a plan called Skill India, hoping to provide 500 million people with skills by 2022.

01:10:48.466 --> 01:10:54.232
But this ambitious project met many obstacles and has now been abandoned.

01:10:54.291 --> 01:11:02.870
At the moment, it's impractical because so many people don't always have access to schools, banks and other services.

01:11:02.930 --> 01:11:12.728
But the digital age brings hope, as more services and education are available online, so maybe things will improve in the future.

01:11:12.788 --> 01:11:19.352
Despite all these obstacles, India attracts larger numbers of tourists every year.

01:11:19.412 --> 01:11:34.486
It's difficult not to be captivated by India's spectacular landscapes and architecture, exotic cuisine and incredible spirituality.

01:11:34.546 --> 01:11:50.916
What did John Lennon from the English band The Beatles have in common with Mira Afasa, a French-born spiritual leader known as the Mother?

01:11:50.976 --> 01:12:01.665
Both imagined a world without any countries, nothing to kill or die for, no religion and people living life in peace.

01:12:01.725 --> 01:12:13.040
The mother's vision led to the founding of Orville, City of Dawn, an experimental town in Tamil Nadu, India.

01:12:13.100 --> 01:12:22.796
Alfasa founded Orville in 1968, transforming a useless piece of land into a green forest.

01:12:22.856 --> 01:12:31.007
Her dream was to promote human unity, practice sustainable living and provide for the future.

01:12:31.067 --> 01:12:41.200
Today, a community of around 2,500 people from 49 nations is trying to make her dream a reality.

01:12:41.260 --> 01:12:45.443
Orville is trying to be a self-sufficient town.

01:12:45.503 --> 01:12:53.442
Farms produce organic food and energy systems generate solar, wind and gas energy.

01:12:53.502 --> 01:13:03.559
The people who live in Oraville leave a unique lifestyle without politics or religion, which they believe can divide people.

01:13:03.619 --> 01:13:06.692
But not everything is perfect in Auravil.

01:13:06.752 --> 01:13:14.580
The original idea was for the population to work for the community and for the community to take care of them.

01:13:14.640 --> 01:13:17.364
But this hasn't worked too well.

01:13:17.424 --> 01:13:20.978
Auravilians need help from volunteers.

01:13:21.039 --> 01:13:24.341
Otherwise, they can't maintain the community.

01:13:24.401 --> 01:13:35.524
Although everybody receives basic health care and education, some people have found ways to make extra money and live a better lifestyle.

01:13:35.584 --> 01:13:46.830
Despite the problems, the dream is still alive to have a community that doesn't waste the Earth's resources and lives in peace and harmony.

01:13:46.890 --> 01:13:48.860
Would you like to live there?

01:13:48.920 --> 01:13:55.392
Page 80 Cross-curricular focus the yoga community.

01:13:55.452 --> 01:14:03.868
From Hollywood film stars to the New Zealand rugby players, yoga is becoming more and more popular.

01:14:03.928 --> 01:14:10.152
In fact, in the USA there are more yoga studios than Starbucks coffee shops.

01:14:10.212 --> 01:14:19.695
Today, people in the western world practice yoga to improve flexibility, alleviate stress and get physically fit.

01:14:19.755 --> 01:14:31.600
But 5,000 years ago, people from the Indusarasvati civilization practiced yoga for a different reason, to discover their true nature.

01:14:31.660 --> 01:14:35.595
To do this, people tried to control their thoughts.

01:14:35.656 --> 01:14:39.867
We all think thoughts, and they control how we feel.

01:14:39.927 --> 01:14:43.498
They can make us feel happy or depressed.

01:14:43.558 --> 01:14:54.040
By practicing yoga, people focus on their thoughts for a long period of time and can begin to understand more about themselves.

01:14:54.100 --> 01:15:03.502
The special technique of controlling your thoughts is called meditation, and it existed as early as 1500 BC.

01:15:03.562 --> 01:15:10.126
In ancient times, people went to an ashram, meaning a shelter.

01:15:10.186 --> 01:15:14.098
In those days, the ashram was the house of the guru.

01:15:14.158 --> 01:15:22.324
A guru is a spiritual teacher who guides his or her disciples to a deeper understanding of themselves.

01:15:22.384 --> 01:15:31.699
In the past, children as young as five went to the guru's house to learn how to behave like Brahman, the one true God.

01:15:31.759 --> 01:15:36.174
Can you imagine leaving your parents at the age of five?

01:15:36.234 --> 01:15:42.374
Very talented children graduated and received a Brahmana degree.

01:15:42.434 --> 01:15:48.534
In the course of time, these children became gurus and went on to teach others.

01:15:48.594 --> 01:15:54.112
But it was a very slow process and often took more than 20 years.

